window.LESSONS.push({
 id:'a6-2',area:6,title:'Math, dates, and random choices',objectives:['6.4','6.5','6.6'],
 sections:[
 {heading:'Math functions and constants',body:`<p>Import <code>math</code> for mathematical functions and constants. <code>fabs(x)</code> returns a floating-point absolute value. <code>ceil</code> rounds toward positive infinity; <code>floor</code> toward negative infinity; and <code>trunc</code> discards the fractional part toward zero. These differ for negative values.</p><pre><code>import math
math.ceil(2.1)       # 3
math.floor(-2.1)     # -3
math.trunc(-2.1)     # -2
math.fmod(7.0, 3.0)  # 1.0
math.frexp(8.0)      # (0.5, 4)
math.sqrt(81)        # 9.0
math.isqrt(81)       # 9
math.pow(2, 3)       # 8.0
math.pi              # circle constant</code></pre><p><code>fmod(x, y)</code> computes a floating-point remainder with the sign of x (unlike the % operator in some negative cases). <code>frexp(x)</code> returns mantissa and exponent such that x = mantissa × 2**exponent. <code>nan</code> is a not-a-number value; compare it with <code>math.isnan(x)</code>, because NaN is not equal to itself. <code>sqrt</code> accepts nonnegative real values; <code>isqrt</code> gives the floor of the exact integer square root for nonnegative integers.</p>`},
 {heading:'Datetime formatting and weekday',body:`<p>The <code>datetime</code> module provides date and time objects. <code>datetime.now()</code> returns the current local date and time as a datetime object (with no timezone attached by default). Its value changes from call to call. Use <code>strftime</code> to format it using directives such as <code>%Y</code> (four-digit year), <code>%m</code> (month), <code>%d</code> (day), <code>%H</code> (24-hour), <code>%M</code> (minute), and <code>%A</code> (weekday name).</p><pre><code>from datetime import datetime
now = datetime.now()
label = now.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M")
weekday_index = now.weekday()  # Monday 0 ... Sunday 6</code></pre><p><code>weekday()</code> returns an integer: Monday is 0 and Sunday is 6. Do not confuse it with <code>isoweekday()</code>, where Monday is 1 and Sunday is 7. A formatting string is a template; it does not change the underlying datetime.</p>`},
 {heading:'Random numbers and selections',body:`<p>The <code>random</code> module generates pseudo-random values. <code>random()</code> returns a float in the half-open interval [0.0, 1.0). <code>randrange(start, stop[, step])</code> chooses from the corresponding range; the stop is excluded. <code>randint(a, b)</code> includes both endpoints. <code>choice(seq)</code> selects one element, <code>sample(population, k)</code> selects k distinct elements without replacement, and <code>shuffle(list)</code> reorders a mutable sequence in place and returns <code>None</code>.</p><pre><code>import random
r = random.random()                 # 0.0 <= r < 1.0
n = random.randrange(2, 8)          # 2 through 7
m = random.randint(2, 8)            # 2 through 8
one = random.choice(["red", "blue"])
picks = random.sample(range(10), 3) # distinct values
cards = ["A", "K", "Q"]
result = random.shuffle(cards)      # cards changes; result is None</code></pre><p>Random outputs vary, so reason about ranges and guarantees rather than a particular draw. For repeatable experiments, a seeded <code>random.Random(seed)</code> instance provides reproducibility in a given Python implementation/version context; do not use this module for security-sensitive tokens.</p>`}
 ],
 sources:[{label:'math — Mathematical functions',url:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/math.html'},{label:'datetime — Basic date and time types',url:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/datetime.html'},{label:'random — Generate pseudo-random numbers',url:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/random.html'}],
 questions:[
 {"id": "a6-2-q1", "objective": "6.4", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>For each statement about the following code, select True or False.</p><pre><code>import math</code></pre><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "<code>type(abs(-3)) is float</code>", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>type(math.fabs(-3)) is float</code>", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "<code>math.fabs(-3.5) == 3.5</code>", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<p>math.fabs always returns a float, even for an integer argument, whereas the built-in abs keeps the argument type and returns int 3 for -3.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q2", "objective": "6.5", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<pre><code>import math\nprint(math.ceil(-2.3))</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value -3 is displayed.", "The value -2 is displayed.", "The value -2.0 is displayed.", "The value 3 is displayed."], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "<p>ceil rounds toward positive infinity, and -2 is greater than -2.3. It returns an int, not a float, so -2.0 is wrong.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q3", "objective": "6.6", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You need to determine the value returned by each call to <code>math.floor</code>.</p><p>Move the appropriate result from the list on the left to each call on the right.</p><p>You may use each item once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["2", "-2", "-3", "3", "-1", "4"], "parts": [{"label": "<code>math.floor(-2.3)</code>", "options": ["2", "-2", "-3", "3", "-1", "4"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "<code>math.floor(2.7)</code>", "options": ["2", "-2", "-3", "3", "-1", "4"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "<code>math.floor(-0.5)</code>", "options": ["2", "-2", "-3", "3", "-1", "4"], "answer": 4}, {"label": "<code>math.floor(4.0)</code>", "options": ["2", "-2", "-3", "3", "-1", "4"], "answer": 5}], "explanation": "<p>floor rounds toward negative infinity, so negative values move away from zero: -2.3 becomes -3 and -0.5 becomes -1. Positive values simply lose the fraction.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q4", "objective": "6.4", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>You have the following variable.</p><pre><code>import math\nx = -2.9</code></pre><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "<code>math.trunc(x) == math.floor(x)</code>", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>math.trunc(x) == -2</code>", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "<code>math.trunc(2.9) == math.floor(2.9)</code>", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<p>trunc discards the fraction toward zero, while floor moves toward negative infinity. They agree for positive numbers and differ for negative non-integers.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q5", "objective": "6.5", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<pre><code>import math\nprint(math.fmod(-7.0, 3.0))\nprint(-7.0 % 3.0)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The values -1.0 and 2.0 are displayed.", "The values 2.0 and 2.0 are displayed.", "The values -1.0 and -1.0 are displayed.", "The values 2.0 and -1.0 are displayed."], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<p>math.fmod gives the remainder with the sign of the first argument, so it is -1.0. The % operator takes the sign of the divisor, giving 2.0.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q6", "objective": "6.6", "kind": "input", "prompt": "<pre><code>import math\nm, e = math.frexp(8.0)\nprint(e)</code></pre><p>What value will print? Enter the number as an integer here:</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Enter the number as an integer", "input": true, "answer": ["4"]}], "explanation": "<p>frexp returns (mantissa, exponent) with 0.5 <= mantissa < 1, so 8.0 = 0.5 * 2**4 and the exponent is 4. The mantissa is 0.5.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q7", "objective": "6.4", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>You are writing a program that must display the value <code>7.0</code>, the square root of 49.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "{{0}} math\n\nresult = math.{{1}}(49)\nprint(result)  # 7.0", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["include", "using", "import", "from"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Drop-down 2", "options": ["isqrt", "fabs", "pow", "sqrt"], "answer": 3}], "explanation": "<p>sqrt returns a float, so it prints 7.0, whereas isqrt returns the integer 7. The module must be imported before math.sqrt can be used.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q8", "objective": "6.5", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<pre><code>import math\nprint(math.isqrt(27))</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value 5.196152422706632 is displayed.", "The value 5 is displayed.", "The value 6 is displayed.", "The value 5.0 is displayed."], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "<p>isqrt returns the floor of the exact square root as an int. It does not round up and it does not return a float.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q9", "objective": "6.6", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You need to determine the value of each expression.</p><p>Move the appropriate result from the list on the left to each expression on the right.</p><p>You may use each item once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["25.0", "25", "0.5", "32.0", "32"], "parts": [{"label": "<code>math.pow(2, 5)</code>", "options": ["25.0", "25", "0.5", "32.0", "32"], "answer": 3}, {"label": "<code>2 ** 5</code>", "options": ["25.0", "25", "0.5", "32.0", "32"], "answer": 4}, {"label": "<code>math.pow(5, 2)</code>", "options": ["25.0", "25", "0.5", "32.0", "32"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "<code>pow(2, -1)</code>", "options": ["25.0", "25", "0.5", "32.0", "32"], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "<p>math.pow always returns a float, while ** and the built-in pow keep integers when the exponent is a non-negative int. A negative exponent makes the built-in pow return a float.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q10", "objective": "6.4", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<pre><code>import math\nprint(type(math.pi).__name__, round(math.pi, 2))</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The text float 3.14 is displayed.", "The text function 3.14 is displayed.", "The text int 3 is displayed.", "A TypeError occurs because pi must be called as math.pi()."], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<p>math.pi is a float constant, not a function, so it is used without parentheses. round(..., 2) gives 3.14.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q11", "objective": "6.5", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>The program must display <code>not a number</code> when <code>x</code> holds NaN.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "{{0}} math\n\nx = float(\"nan\")\nif math.{{1}}(x):\n    print(\"not a number\")\nelse:\n    print(\"number\")", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["using", "include", "require", "import"], "answer": 3}, {"label": "Drop-down 2", "options": ["isinf", "isfinite", "isnan", "nan"], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "<p>isnan is the dedicated test for NaN. isinf and isfinite answer different questions and are False for NaN, and math.nan is a value, not a callable function.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q12", "objective": "6.6", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>You have the following variable.</p><pre><code>import math\nn = float(\"nan\")</code></pre><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "<code>math.isnan(n)</code> is <code>True</code>", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "<code>n == n</code> is <code>True</code>", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>n != n</code> is <code>True</code>", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<p>NaN is not equal to anything, including itself, so == is False and != is True. That is why math.isnan is used to detect it.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q13", "objective": "6.4", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<pre><code>import math\nprint(math.sqrt(-1))</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The value nan is displayed.", "The value 1j is displayed.", "The value -1.0 is displayed.", "A ValueError exception is raised."], "answer": 3}], "explanation": "<p>math.sqrt works only with real, non-negative inputs and raises ValueError for -1. Complex results require the cmath module.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q14", "objective": "6.5", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You need to determine the value returned by each call.</p><p>Move the appropriate result from the list on the left to each call on the right.</p><p>You may use each item once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["-4", "-5", "5", "4"], "parts": [{"label": "<code>math.floor(-4.8)</code>", "options": ["-4", "-5", "5", "4"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>math.trunc(-4.8)</code>", "options": ["-4", "-5", "5", "4"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "<code>math.ceil(4.8)</code>", "options": ["-4", "-5", "5", "4"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "<code>math.trunc(4.8)</code>", "options": ["-4", "-5", "5", "4"], "answer": 3}], "explanation": "<p>For negative numbers floor goes down to -5 while trunc goes toward zero to -4. For positive numbers ceil rounds up to 5 and trunc drops the fraction to 4.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q15", "objective": "6.6", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>You have the following code.</p><pre><code>from datetime import date\nd = date(2025, 3, 3)  # a Monday</code></pre><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "<code>d.isoweekday() == 0</code>", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>d.weekday() == 0</code>", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "<code>d.strftime(\"%A\") == \"Monday\"</code>", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<p>weekday numbers Monday as 0, but isoweekday numbers Monday as 1. %A formats the full day name.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q16", "objective": "6.4", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>You need to display the date as four-digit year, month and day separated by hyphens.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "from datetime import datetime\n\nd = datetime(2025, 3, 7, 14, 30)\nprint(d.{{0}}(\"{{1}}\"))  # 2025-03-07", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["strptime", "weekday", "strftime", "isoformat"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Drop-down 2", "options": ["%Y-%m-%d", "%y-%M-%D", "%Y-%d-%m", "Y-m-d"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<p>strftime formats a datetime using percent directives: %Y year, %m month, %d day. Letters without % are printed literally, and %M is minutes, not month.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q17", "objective": "6.5", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<pre><code>from datetime import datetime\nstamp = datetime.now()\nprint(type(stamp).__name__, type(stamp.strftime(\"%Y\")).__name__)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The text str str is displayed.", "The text datetime str is displayed.", "The text datetime int is displayed.", "The text float str is displayed."], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "<p>now returns a datetime object. strftime formats it into a string, even when the directive looks numeric like %Y.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q18", "objective": "6.6", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<pre><code>from datetime import date\ndays = [\"Mon\", \"Tue\", \"Wed\", \"Thu\", \"Fri\", \"Sat\", \"Sun\"]\nprint(days[date(2025, 3, 9).weekday()])</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The text Sat is displayed.", "The text Sun is displayed.", "The text Mon is displayed.", "An IndexError occurs."], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "<p>March 9, 2025 is a Sunday, and weekday returns 6 for Sunday, which indexes the last list item. With isoweekday the value 7 would raise IndexError.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q19", "objective": "6.4", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>You are writing a Python program that uses <code>random.random()</code>.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "The value returned can be <code>1.0</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "The value returned is always a float.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "The value returned can be <code>0.0</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<p>random.random() returns a float in the half-open range 0.0 <= x < 1.0, so 0.0 is possible and 1.0 is excluded.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q20", "objective": "6.5", "kind": "multi", "prompt": "<p>A game development company needs to generate a random number. The number must meet the following requirements:</p><ul><li>The number is an integer.</li><li>The lowest number is 3.</li><li>The highest number is 6.</li></ul><p>Which two code segments generate a random number that meets the requirements? (Choose 2.) Assume <code>import random</code> has been run.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Choose two.", "options": ["random.randint(3, 6)", "random.randrange(3, 7)", "random.random() * 3 + 3", "random.randint(3, 7)", "random.randrange(3, 6)"], "answer": [0, 1], "multi": true}], "explanation": "<p>randrange excludes its stop value, so randrange(3, 7) yields 3 through 6, while randint includes both endpoints. The other choices top out at 5, top out at 7, or return a float.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q21", "objective": "6.6", "kind": "tf", "prompt": "<p>You are writing a Python program. Assume <code>import random</code> has been run.</p><p>For each statement, select True or False.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "<code>random.randrange(3, 7)</code> can return <code>7</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>random.randint(3, 7)</code> can return <code>7</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "<code>random.randrange(3, 7)</code> can return <code>3</code>.", "options": ["True", "False"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<p>randint(a, b) includes both a and b. randrange(a, b) includes the start but excludes the stop, as with range.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q22", "objective": "6.4", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>The program must select one color name at random from the list and display <code>str</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "{{0}}\n\ncolors = [\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\"]\npick = random.{{1}}(colors)\nprint(type(pick).__name__)  # str", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["from random import choice", "from random import random", "import random", "import random.choice"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Drop-down 2", "options": ["choice", "sample", "shuffle", "randint"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<p>import random makes the name random available, so random.choice(colors) works and returns one element. shuffle returns None, sample needs a count, and the from-import forms never bind the name random as a module.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q23", "objective": "6.5", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<p>A quiz program stores ten student names in a list named <code>names</code>. The program must select four different students at random. Assume <code>import random</code> has been run.</p><p>Which code segment should you use?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["[random.choice(names) for _ in range(4)]", "random.shuffle(names)[:4]", "random.randint(0, 4)", "random.sample(names, 4)"], "answer": 3}], "explanation": "<p>sample picks k unique elements without replacement. Repeated choice calls can return the same student twice, and shuffle returns None, so slicing it raises TypeError.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q24", "objective": "6.6", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<pre><code>import random\ncards = [\"A\", \"K\", \"Q\"]\nresult = random.shuffle(cards)\nprint(result)</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["A shuffled list such as ['K', 'A', 'Q'] is displayed.", "The value 3 is displayed.", "A TypeError occurs.", "The text None is displayed."], "answer": 3}], "explanation": "<p>shuffle reorders the list in place and returns None. Print cards, not the return value, to see the new order.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q25", "objective": "6.4", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You have the following list.</p><pre><code>import random\nnames = [\"Ana\", \"Ben\", \"Cy\"]</code></pre><p>You need to identify the type of the value each call returns. Move the appropriate type from the list on the left to each call on the right.</p><p>You may use each item once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["float", "str", "list", "int", "NoneType"], "parts": [{"label": "<code>type(random.sample(names, 2))</code>", "options": ["float", "str", "list", "int", "NoneType"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "<code>type(random.choice(names))</code>", "options": ["float", "str", "list", "int", "NoneType"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>type(random.shuffle(names))</code>", "options": ["float", "str", "list", "int", "NoneType"], "answer": 4}, {"label": "<code>type(random.randint(1, 6))</code>", "options": ["float", "str", "list", "int", "NoneType"], "answer": 3}], "explanation": "<p>sample returns a new list, choice returns one element (a str here), and randint returns an int. shuffle works in place and returns None, whose type is NoneType.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q26", "objective": "6.5", "kind": "drag", "prompt": "<p>You need to identify the possible results of each call. Assume <code>import random</code> has been run.</p><p>Move the appropriate range from the list on the left to each call on the right.</p><p>You may use each item once, more than once, or not at all.</p>", "bank": ["0.0 up to, but not including, 1.0", "1 through 5", "1 through 6", "0, 5, or 10", "0 or 5"], "parts": [{"label": "<code>random.randint(1, 6)</code>", "options": ["0.0 up to, but not including, 1.0", "1 through 5", "1 through 6", "0, 5, or 10", "0 or 5"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "<code>random.randrange(1, 6)</code>", "options": ["0.0 up to, but not including, 1.0", "1 through 5", "1 through 6", "0, 5, or 10", "0 or 5"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "<code>random.randrange(0, 10, 5)</code>", "options": ["0.0 up to, but not including, 1.0", "1 through 5", "1 through 6", "0, 5, or 10", "0 or 5"], "answer": 4}, {"label": "<code>random.random()</code>", "options": ["0.0 up to, but not including, 1.0", "1 through 5", "1 through 6", "0, 5, or 10", "0 or 5"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<p>randint includes its upper bound, randrange does not, and a step of 5 from 0 up to but excluding 10 gives 0 and 5. random() returns a float below 1.0.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q27", "objective": "6.6", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>You are writing a program that must generate a random number. The number must be 2, 4, 6, or 8.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "import random\n\n# The number must be 2, 4, 6, or 8\nn = random.randrange({{0}}, {{1}}, {{2}})", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["0", "2", "1", "4"], "answer": 1}, {"label": "Drop-down 2", "options": ["8", "12", "10", "6"], "answer": 2}, {"label": "Drop-down 3", "options": ["2", "1", "4", "3"], "answer": 0}], "explanation": "<p>randrange(start, stop, step) excludes stop. Starting at 2 with a step of 2 and a stop of 10 produces 2, 4, 6 and 8; a stop of 8 would drop 8.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q28", "objective": "6.4", "kind": "mc", "prompt": "<pre><code>from datetime import datetime\nd = datetime(2025, 3, 7)\nprint(d.strftime(\"%A\"))</code></pre><p>You run the code. What is the result?</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Select one answer", "options": ["The text Fri is displayed.", "The text Friday is displayed.", "The value 4 is displayed.", "The text March is displayed."], "answer": 1}], "explanation": "<p>%A is the full weekday name, whereas %a is the abbreviation. weekday would give the number 4, and %B gives the month name.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q29", "objective": "6.5", "kind": "input", "prompt": "<pre><code>from datetime import datetime\nd = datetime(2025, 3, 7, 14, 5, 9)\nprint(int(d.strftime(\"%M\")) * int(d.strftime(\"%m\")))</code></pre><p>What value will print? Enter the number as an integer here:</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Enter the number as an integer", "input": true, "answer": ["15"]}], "explanation": "<p>%M is minutes (5) and lowercase %m is month (3), so the product is 15. Mixing up the two directives is the usual trap.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q30", "objective": "6.6", "kind": "multi", "prompt": "<p>You are writing a program that generates a password-reset token. The token must be unpredictable to an attacker (cryptographically secure).</p><p>Which two code segments should you use? (Choose 2.) Assume the needed modules have been imported.</p>", "parts": [{"label": "Choose two.", "options": ["random.choice(string.ascii_letters)", "random.randint(100000, 999999)", "secrets.token_hex(16)", "secrets.token_urlsafe(32)", "random.Random(1234).random()"], "answer": [2, 3], "multi": true}], "explanation": "<p>The secrets module uses the operating system source of cryptographic randomness. The random module is a deterministic pseudo-random generator intended for simulation, and its documentation warns against using it for security purposes.</p>"},
 {"id": "a6-2-q31", "objective": "6.4", "kind": "dropdown", "prompt": "<p>You are writing a program that computes the circumference and area of a circle with radius <code>r</code>.</p><p>Complete the code by selecting the correct code segment from each drop-down list.</p>", "template": "import math\n\nr = 5\ncircumference = 2 * {{0}} * r  # about 31.4\narea = math.pi * {{1}}  # about 78.5", "parts": [{"label": "Drop-down 1", "options": ["math.pi", "math.e", "math.tau", "math.inf"], "answer": 0}, {"label": "Drop-down 2", "options": ["r * 2", "math.sqrt(r)", "r ** 2", "2 * r"], "answer": 2}], "explanation": "<p>math.pi supplies the constant, and the area formula is pi times the radius squared. r * 2 and 2 * r compute a diameter, and math.tau is 2 pi, which would double the circumference.</p>"},
 ]
});
